            'When the right foot is on the ground, body weight tends to lower the left side of the pelvis. The right gluteus medius and minimus oppose this effect and help keep the pelvis close to horizontal. The abductors doing their work without a visible abduction movement while standing shows that muscle function is not limited to lifting the free limb sideways.',
            'The gluteus maximus supports hip extension when straightening from a sitting position; the requirement in calm walking on level ground and in climbing stairs is not the same. While the iliopsoas carries the thigh forwards the trunk must be stabilised; when the femur is fixed its effect can be directed at the trunk. The deep external rotators complete the control around the head of the femur. The relationship of the tensor fasciae latae with the iliotibial tract is an example of a muscle contributing to the balance of a more distant joint through a fascial connection.',
