            'The quadriceps femoris consists of the rectus femoris, vastus medialis, lateralis and intermedius. Because the rectus femoris arises from the pelvis it also takes part in hip flexion; the vasti arise from the femur and produce their basic extensor effect only at the knee. The common tendon reaches the patella and the patellar ligament the tibial tuberosity. The sartorius, with its oblique course, combines hip flexion–abduction–external rotation with knee flexion.',
            'Most of the hamstrings arise from the ischial tuberosity and cross both the hip and the knee. The short head of the biceps femoris is the exception to this general pattern because it arises from the femur. The pes anserinus is the common regional attachment of the sartorius, gracilis and semitendinosus tendons on the medial tibia; the three muscles come from different compartments of the thigh.',
