            'Because the gastrocnemius crosses the knee, its length changes when the knee bends; the proximal end of the soleus lies below the knee and does not experience this effect in the same way. The lateral fibularis muscles, with their pull in the direction of eversion, balance the muscles that invert on the medial side. Their contracting together does not necessarily rotate the foot in two directions at once but can limit unwanted sideways movement. Following whether the tendons pass in front of or behind the malleoli helps in understanding the direction of movement.',
