        'The four parts of the quadriceps femoris are the rectus femoris, vastus lateralis, vastus medialis and vastus intermedius. All four take part in knee extension; because the rectus femoris also crosses the hip joint it additionally contributes to hip flexion. The vasti arise from the femur and do not cross the hip joint. You therefore cannot replace the phrase “it extends the thigh” with “it extends the knee”. The rectus femoris is a flexor at the hip and an extensor at the knee.',
        'The sartorius arises from the spina iliaca anterior superior and extends to the superomedial tibia; it produces flexion, abduction and external rotation at the hip and flexion at the knee. The gracilis is the thin long muscle passing from the pubis to the medial tibia; it takes part in hip adduction and knee flexion. The sartorius, gracilis and semitendinosus form the pes anserinus. When recognising the gracilis in a figure, follow the long strip running along the medial thigh and crossing the knee; do not expect the same appearance as the short, broad pectineus.',
        'Most of the hamstrings arise from the tuber ischiadicum and produce extension at the hip and flexion at the knee. The short head of the biceps femoris arises from the femur and therefore does not cross the hip. The adductor and hamstring parts of the adductor magnus carry different functional and neural features; a single absolute movement label does not explain the whole muscle. The reliable way of knowing whether a muscle crosses two joints is to look at which joints the line between its two attachments passes.',
