        'The anterior leg compartment is related to the n. fibularis profundus; the tibialis anterior, extensor hallucis longus and extensor digitorum longus are its main muscles. They take part in dorsiflexion of the ankle; the long extensors also extend the relevant toes. The tibialis anterior produces inversion together with dorsiflexion. Laterally the fibularis longus and brevis receive the n. fibularis superficialis and take part in eversion; they can assist plantar flexion. Do not write these two fibular nerves as a single branch of the same name.',
        'The posterior compartment is related to the n. tibialis. Superficially the gastrocnemius and soleus establish a powerful plantar flexor arrangement; together they are called the triceps surae and extend to the calcaneus through the calcaneal (Achilles) tendon. Because the gastrocnemius also passes behind the knee it takes part in knee flexion; the soleus arises from the tibia–fibula and does not cross the knee. The deep tibialis posterior produces plantar flexion and inversion and supports the medial arch. Because its name is tibialis it is not assumed to perform the same movement as the anterior one.',
